### 5.5. Why can atomic operations be slower?

Atomic operations can reduce performance because updates to the same memory address must be serialized when multiple threads contend for that address.

In this experiment, all threads update `d_total[0]`. This creates heavy contention for one memory location. Threads cannot all complete their updates independently at the same instant.

The naive implementation may appear faster because it does not coordinate concurrent updates, but its result can be incorrect. The atomic implementation prioritizes correctness and ensures that updates are not lost.

