## 8. Conclusion

This laboratory work demonstrated important characteristics of GPU programming using CUDA Python and Numba.

First, hardware inspection confirmed that the experiment was performed on an NVIDIA Tesla T4 GPU with compute capability 7.5, a maximum of 1024 threads per block, and a warp size of 32 threads.

Second, the CPU-versus-GPU benchmark showed that GPU acceleration does not automatically improve performance for small workloads. The empirical crossover point was 10,000 elements. At smaller sizes, GPU execution and data transfer overhead outweighed the benefits of parallel processing. At 10,000 elements and above, the measured GPU total time was lower than the CPU time for the sizes where CPU measurements were available.

Third, the safe CUDA kernel correctly processed all 1,000 elements using four blocks of 256 threads. The boundary condition prevented 24 excess threads from accessing invalid array indices.

The thread-coordinate experiment used 37 elements and 8 threads per block. It required five blocks and 40 threads, of which 37 were active and three were guarded. This demonstrated how global thread IDs map to block and thread coordinates.

Fourth, the reduction example illustrated why unsynchronized concurrent updates can produce race conditions. Atomic operations provide a correct mechanism for updating a shared value, although contention can reduce performance.

Finally, the two-dimensional vignette kernel demonstrated how a CUDA grid can map threads to matrix coordinates. The expected grid configuration was 64 × 64 blocks, with 256 threads per block, and the expected intensity values were 1.000 at the center and 0.000 at the outer corner.

Overall, the experiment demonstrated that effective GPU programming requires appropriate workload size, correct thread geometry, safe memory access, synchronization, and careful measurement of both computation and data transfer overhead.
